 The profiler and allocation-trace SAMPLE BUFFERS live in the free
 low-memory band above the AP idle stacks and below the EBDA
 ([0x60000, 0x9FC00)). They were originally placed at 36160 and
 560464, where the profiler buffer sat inside the PML4 page and grew
 across the live PDPT and PDs -- enabling the profiler destroyed the
 page tables after ~88 samples -- and the trace buffer grew across the
 EBDA and VGA text memory. Control cells (enabled/cursor) are 8-byte
 slots and stay in the metadata band; only the buffers needed moving.
 codex-vm's -trace-file reader mirrors the trace addresses.

  prof-max-samples : Integer = 8192
  prof-buf-addr : Integer = 393216
  trace-enabled-addr : Integer = 458752
  trace-cursor-addr : Integer = 458760
  trace-buf-addr : Integer = 458768
  trace-max-entries : Integer = 12000
  serial-ring-buf-addr : Integer = 5242880

 The page an application processor begins executing in. A start-up IPI's
 vector field names a 4 KB page below 1 MB, and the processor enters it in
 REAL MODE with CS:IP = vector:0000 -- so this is a page of 16-bit code, not
 a cell holding a 64-bit address, which is what it used to be. See
 ap-tramp-blob.

  ap-trampoline-addr : Integer = ap-sipi-vector * 4096
  ap-core-count-addr : Integer = 4088
  ap-ready-count-addr : Integer = 4080
  ap-stack-table-addr : Integer = 3840
  smp-max-cores : Integer = 16

 How many processes an application processor has claimed and run. Only a
 core whose id is non-zero adds to this cell, and the boot processor's id
 is always zero, so no path the BSP can take reaches it and no host code
 writes it. That is the whole reason it exists: a count above zero is
 evidence that a core other than the boot processor took a process out of
 the table and executed it, and that is the one thing a test of this
 cannot take on trust. Read by codex/test/smp-dispatch.codex.

 Cell 36200, and not a tidier address: 36152 is a permanent booby trap (a
 legacy codex-vm output-ring position) and 36160 / 36168 / 36176 are
 codex-vm's blit cells. Grep tools/codex-vm.c before claiming any cell in
 this band.

  ap-dispatch-count-addr : Integer = 36200

 The UEFI system table pointer, snapshotted before we destroy it.

 A UEFI stub hands the kernel its system table by leaving the pointer at
 GPA 0x8000, and every uefi-* helper used to read that address directly.
 0x8000 is also where emit-build-process-page-tables puts the PML4, and
 emit-start loads CR3 with it. So by the time any helper ran, the pointer
 it read was page-table entry zero -- it dereferenced that as a system
 table and called through it, which is an invalid opcode fault at a low
 address, on every boot, in every mode. It cannot ever have worked.

 emit-start copies [0x8000] here BEFORE emit-process-setup overwrites it,
 and the helpers read this cell instead. On a boot with no UEFI stub the
 snapshot is zero and every helper returns -1 rather than calling into the
 page tables. A helper that cannot reach firmware must say so, not jump
 into it.

 THE COPY ONLY HAPPENS WHEN THIS CELL IS STILL ZERO, and that condition is
 load-bearing rather than an optimisation. cdx-to-pe.ps1 writes the pointer
 to BOTH 0x8000 and this cell before it jumps, because a stub that calls
 opening directly never runs emit-start at all. Under -EntryStart emit-start
 DOES run, and an unconditional copy then overwrote a correct pointer with
 whatever 0x8000 held by then: measured 2026-08-09, this cell read 0 and
 0x8000 read 0x9023, a page-table entry. Every uefi-* helper therefore
 dereferenced null on the one path that boots the compiler from a stick,
 which is why A5 could not read or write its volume on metal.

 Same shape as the serial-ring prefill (serial-primed-magic): a later stage
 must not clobber what an earlier stage deliberately primed. A non-zero cell
 means somebody already knows the answer.

 IT LIVED AT 36208 UNTIL 2026-08-09 AND THAT ADDRESS IS INSIDE THE PAGE
 TABLES. emit-build-process-page-tables puts the PML4 at 0x8000 and a PML4 is
 a full 4 KB, so it owns 0x8000 through 0x8FFF -- and 36208 is 0x8D70, entry
 430 of that table. Building the tables zeroes the page, so anything written
 to this cell BEFORE emit-process-setup is destroyed.

 That is survivable for the rest of the 36xxx band, which is why nothing
 caught it: ap-dispatch-count, spawn-affinity and fs-elevated are all written
 at runtime, long after the tables exist, and they occupy PML4 entries no
 canonical address reaches. This cell is the one exception, because the UEFI
 stub primes it before jumping, and that write is the one the page tables eat.

 Measured under -EntryStart: cell read 0 and 0x8000 read 0x9023, a PML4 entry
 pointing at the PDPT. Every uefi-* helper then dereferenced null, which is
 why the compiler could neither read nor write its own boot volume on a board.

 30704 is below the tables, between serial-primed-magic and chan-table-base,
 and was confirmed unclaimed in tools/codex-vm.c, apps/works and
 codex/foreword as well as here. cdx-to-pe.ps1 carries the same number as
 $UefiSystabAddr and the two must move together.

  uefi-systab-addr : Integer = 30704

 The ImageHandle the firmware passed in RCX at PE entry, primed by the stub
 beside the SystemTable and zeroed with it at ExitBootServices. 30712 is the
 next qword below the tables, confirmed unclaimed in tools/codex-vm.c,
 apps/works, codex/foreword and here. cdx-to-pe.ps1 carries the same number
 as $UefiImgHandleAddr and the two must move together. Zero means no stub
 primed it, and the block helpers then fall back to LocateProtocol.

  uefi-imghandle-addr : Integer = 30712

 Timer interrupts taken by a core whose id is not zero. Only the interrupt
 handler writes it, and only when the core it is running on is not the boot
 processor, so a value above zero is evidence that an application processor
 was preempted -- that a core other than the BSP took a scheduling clock
 tick and could be forced off whatever it was running. Read by
 codex/test/smp-preempt.codex.

 A count of finished work would prove nothing here, exactly as it proved
 nothing for AP dispatch: one core can finish the work, and a core that is
 never preempted still completes a process that yields. The claim is about
 who was interrupted, so the evidence has to be about who was interrupted.

 Cell 36216, the next free slot above uefi-systab. Grep tools/codex-vm.c
 before claiming any cell in this band.

  ap-preempt-count-addr : Integer = 36216

 The core a spawn pins its child to, handed from process-spawn-on-core to
 process-spawn through one cell. process-spawn reads it into the child's
 affinity field and resets the cell to -1 (any core) for the next spawn, so
 process-spawn-on-core needs no copy of the ninety-line spawn body. The cell
 holds -1 at rest, so an ordinary process-spawn pins to no core. This carries
 the same single-writer assumption the slot finder above it already makes --
 the finder reads and claims a slot without a lock -- so two cores spawning at
 the same instant is out of scope here as it is there.

 Cell 36224, the next free slot above ap-preempt-count. Grep tools/codex-vm.c
 before claiming any cell in this band.

  spawn-affinity-addr : Integer = 36224

 Cell 36232, the next free slot above spawn-affinity. Grepped
 tools/codex-vm.c before claiming: 36152 is the reserved output-ring
 trap, 36160/36168 are the host blit cells, 36200/36208/36216 are
 guest-only counters, and 36224 is spawn-affinity; nothing host-side
 touches 36232.

 fs-elevated is the kernel filesystem servicer's block-authority token.
 It is non-zero for exactly the span in which the
 compiler-installed FileSystem default handler is discharging a
 verified read-text/write-file over fat16, and zero everywhere else.
 The block syscalls (10-13) proceed when the current process holds
 cap-block-device OR this cell is non-zero, which is what lets the
 servicer drive the disk on behalf of a process that carries only
 FileSystem.Read and never Device.Block. It is set and cleared ONLY by
 inline code inside that servicer and is deliberately not reachable as a
 builtin -- a callable elevation primitive would be a universal
 capability bypass (elevate, then block-write-sector).

  fs-elevated-addr : Integer = 36232

 The count is incremented on every vector that is not a timer, and the
 second cell holds the last such vector, so a test can name the line it
 programmed and check that line answered. The timer vectors do not touch
 either cell, which is what keeps the host's own periodic tick out of
 the evidence.

 Cells 36240 and 36248, the next free slots above fs-elevated. Grep
 tools/codex-vm.c before claiming any cell in this band.

  devint-count-addr : Integer = 36240
  devint-last-vec-addr : Integer = 36248

 The next core id to hand out. An application processor no longer arrives
 with its id in a register put there by the host -- it starts in real mode
 knowing nothing -- so it takes one for itself with a locked exchange-add
 on this cell as the last act of the trampoline. The boot processor seeds
 it with 1 before the start-up IPI, so ids run 1, 2, 3 ... and the boot
 processor keeps 0.

 A dense counter rather than the local APIC id, deliberately. The id is
 used to index the AP stack table, the per-core idle stacks, the per-core
 TSS descriptors and the IST stacks, all of which are arrays of
 smp-max-cores entries; a LAPIC id is an identifier and not an index, and
 on a machine that numbers its cores sparsely (or above 15) it would run
 off the end of every one of them. Nothing in the tree needs a core's
 hardware identity, only a distinct small number, so the trampoline reads
 no MMIO at all -- which is one fewer thing that has to work before the
 first application processor can speak.

 Cell 36256, the next free slot above devint-last-vec. Grep
 tools/codex-vm.c before claiming any cell in this band.

  ap-id-next-addr : Integer = 36256

 36264 through 36319 are the NIC control block (`codex/os/net/NetDriver.codex`,
 `net-driver-cb`), so the next free scalar in this band is 36320 and NOT the
 8 bytes above `ap-id-next-addr`. That gap is the one this section's own
 instruction ("find the gap and confirm the preceding allocation has ended")
 exists for, and taking the obvious next slot would have landed a compiler cell
 inside the card's ring pointers.

 The guard page base is PUBLISHED rather than recomputed by its readers.
 `emit-demand-unmap` derives it once from the reported RAM size and stores it
 here; `build` (`Core/PhaseAllocator.codex`) reads it to decide whether a deck
 reservation would leap the page. Two places deriving the same geometry from
 `demand-stack-reserve-pages` is how they drift apart, and a `build` that
 believed the page were higher than it is would let the reservation through.

  guard-page-base-addr : Integer = 36320

 36328 IS THE NIC'S, NOT OURS. `net-driver-poll-cell`
 (`codex/os/net/NetDriver.codex:96`) has held it since the poll interval was
 measured rather than assumed; `net-driver-calibrate` writes it with `poke-32`
 at :70 and `net-driver-poll-interval` reads it at :170. This chapter claimed
 the same cell for `deck-ceiling-addr` on 2026-08-27 and the claim carried the
 words "verified unclaimed against codex-vm.c and net-driver-cb", which is
 exactly as wide as it sounds and no wider: `net-driver-cb` is a BLOCK based at
 36264 declaring offsets 0 and 8, so asking where that block ends answers a
 question about the block and says nothing about a separate scalar the same
 chapter declares sixty lines further down. Both halves of the collision are
 real. `build` writes eight bytes here on every reservation and does it whether
 or not the guard flag is set, so the compiler would silently overwrite the
 measured poll interval with the low half of a deck address, and every bound in
 NetworkStack is denominated in those ticks. In the other direction a
 calibration write drops a small number into the ceiling, every frontier clears
 it, and an armed guard raises `!EXC=06` on the next allocation. The cell the
 newcomer takes is the one that moves.

 The check that finds this shape is a MAP of the band rather than a probe of
 one address: enumerate every literal in [36240, 36420] across all `.codex` and
 `tools/codex-vm.c`, and confirm the known-taken cells appear before believing
 that a candidate does not (L-COUNT, and the negative-result half of
 `check-your-own-instrument`). Run that way, 36336 and 36344 are clear and
 36352 appears only as an unrelated `add-ri` displacement at :1611.

  deck-ceiling-addr : Integer = 36336
  deck-reservation-top-addr : Integer = 36344

 Application-processor idle stacks live in low always-present memory,
 never in the demand-paged heap range: an AP takes its first interrupt
 on this stack, and the CPU cannot deliver a fault frame onto a
 not-present page. Sixteen 16 KB stacks occupy [0x20000, 0x60000),
 below the EBDA and clear of every other low-memory structure. Real
 work on an AP runs on scheduler-provided stacks; these only ever
 carry the idle halt loop and the wake path.

  ap-stack-size : Integer = 16384
  ap-stacks-base : Integer = 131072

  lapic-base : Integer = 4276092928
  lapic-id-reg : Integer = 32
  lapic-eoi-reg : Integer = 176
  lapic-sivr-reg : Integer = 240
  lapic-icr-lo-reg : Integer = 768
  lapic-icr-hi-reg : Integer = 784

 lapic-lvt-timer-reg is the entry in the local vector table (0x320): the
 low eight bits are the vector to raise, bit 16 masks the timer, and bit 17
 selects periodic rather than one-shot. lapic-timer-init-reg (0x380) is the
 count it reloads, and writing zero there disarms it. lapic-timer-div-reg
 (0x3E0) divides the bus clock; 3 is divide-by-16.

  lapic-lvt-timer-reg : Integer = 800
  lapic-timer-init-reg : Integer = 896
  lapic-timer-div-reg : Integer = 992

 The vector an application processor's timer raises. It is deliberately NOT
 32, the PIT's vector: the two clocks are acknowledged differently -- the
 PIT through the 8259 and this through the local APIC -- and a handler that
 could not tell them apart would send the wrong end-of-interrupt to the
 wrong chip.

  lapic-timer-vector : Integer = 48
  lapic-timer-periodic : Integer = 131072
  lapic-timer-divide-16 : Integer = 3
  lapic-timer-count : Integer = 1000000

 The interrupt command register values that start the application
 processors, per the Intel MP protocol: an INIT IPI, then two
 start-up IPIs. Delivery mode sits in bits 8-10 (5 = INIT, 6 = STARTUP),
 the level-assert bit is 14, and destination shorthand 3 in bits 18-19
 means "all excluding self", which is what lets one write reach every AP
 without addressing them one at a time.

  ap-sipi-vector : Integer = 1
  lapic-sivr-enable : Integer = 511
  icr-init-all-but-self : Integer = 804096
  icr-sipi-all-but-self : Integer = 804352 + ap-sipi-vector
  icr-wake-all-but-self : Integer = 802864

 How long the BSP waits for the APs to check in. The wait is bounded: a
 core that never answers must not cost us the boot, so the BSP gives up
 and carries on single-core rather than spinning forever.

  smp-init-delay-cycles : Integer = 100000
  smp-sipi-delay-cycles : Integer = 10000
  smp-wait-fuel : Integer = 50000000

