Section: Process Table Layout

 Which core is running this process. Whoever claims the slot stamps its
 own core id here, so a core can always recover its own identity by
 asking the process it is standing in -- which is the only per-core
 identity the kernel has, and the only one it needs. Offset 8 was free.

  proc-core-offset : Integer = 8
  proc-affinity-offset : Integer = 16

 The network scope: the authority a process is permitted to reach, the
 twin of proc-scope-offset's filesystem path prefix. It lives in the
 reserved gap [24, 56) below the capability word, written by the boot
 grant for proc 0, copied into a child at spawn, and read back by
 process-get-network-scope for the fetch chokepoint to check a runtime
 URL's authority against. Console has no such cell on purpose -- a
 console channel is fixed at compile time by the builtin name and is
 never a runtime value, so there is nothing at runtime to check it
 against.

  proc-net-scope-offset : Integer = 24

 The exit status. Written by the unary process-exit before it joins the
 nullary exit path, and by __proc_entry from the closure's return value
 when a child terminates by returning, so a dead slot always carries a
 defined status; spawn initializes it to zero. Delivered to a waiter by
 the exit wake loop (into the waiter's saved RAX) and read directly by
 process-wait's already-dead and self-rescue paths. Offset 32 is the
 next free slot in the reserved gap [24, 56).

  proc-exit-status-offset : Integer = 32
  proc-cap-offset : Integer = 56
  proc-scope-offset : Integer = 64
  proc-saved-rsp-offset : Integer = 72
  proc-saved-r10-offset : Integer = 80
